  number = {2457468},
  address = {Rochester, NY},
  urldate = {2023-03-09},
  abstract = {The usual legal story about partisan gerrymandering is relentlessly pessimistic. The courts did not even recognize the cause of action until the 1980s; they have never struck down a district plan on this basis; and four sitting Justices want to vacate the field altogether. The Supreme Court's most recent gerrymandering decision, however, is the most encouraging development in this area in a generation. Several Justices expressed interest in the concept of partisan symmetry --- the idea that a plan should treat the major parties symmetrically in terms of the conversion of votes to seats --- and suggested that it could be shaped into a legal test.},
